WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.787
A completion record says
someone finished a course.

2
00:00:02.787 --> 00:00:06.426
Readiness says they can do the
job on the day the change arrives.

3
00:00:06.426 --> 00:00:11.542
Following NIST's learning guide, the
program measures four levels: reaction,

4
00:00:11.542 --> 00:00:15.521
from surveys; learning, from
assessments and labs; behavior,

5
00:00:15.521 --> 00:00:19.420
from help desk ticket trends,
sign-in method registration

6
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.237
and verification audits; and
results, from gate outcomes.

7
00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:25.652
The rule is completion for everyone,

8
00:00:25.652 --> 00:00:29.998
and competence for every role where
a mistake is a security event:

9
00:00:29.998 --> 00:00:33.861
help desk technicians, privileged
administrators, I S S Os,

10
00:00:33.861 --> 00:00:37.563
and the people named in
incident and contingency plans.

11
00:00:37.563 --> 00:00:41.299
For them, completion without a
passing assessment doesn't count.

12
00:00:41.299 --> 00:00:44.913
Registration by wave is the
most direct end-user measure,

13
00:00:44.913 --> 00:00:46.912
and it costs nothing to collect.

14
00:00:46.912 --> 00:00:49.335
A wave that isn't ready moves its date,

15
00:00:49.335 --> 00:00:52.725
not its threshold, and the
pilot sets the baselines.

16
00:00:52.725 --> 00:00:57.118
The steering committee gets one
page at every meeting, in counts,

17
00:00:57.118 --> 00:01:01.990
not names, with privileged gaps as a
count, because each one is a finding.
